# Keep the SSH host key across reboots.
#
# /etc/dropbear is a symlink to /var/run/dropbear, which is a tmpfs, so by
# default dropbear generates a fresh host key on every boot. Every reboot then
# looks like a man-in-the-middle attack to ssh, and the development loop this
# whole stage exists to enable turns into editing known_hosts each time.
#
# So put it in the configuration directory with everything else that has to
# survive a reboot -- see /etc/zlyme.conf, which is also where the caveats about
# the current filesystem live. The -R that S50dropbear already passes means
# dropbear generates the key if the file is absent, so the first boot after
# flashing creates it and every boot after reuses it.
#
# If the writable filesystem did not mount, S15bootpart could not create the
# directory and dropbear falls back to a per-boot key rather than refusing to
# start.
#
# Every boot will still print "No persistent location to store SSH host keys",
# and it is wrong. S50dropbear prints that whenever /etc/dropbear is a symlink it
# cannot remove, without ever looking at DROPBEAR_ARGS, so it cannot know about the
# -r below. Verified on the device: dropbear runs with this argument and the key
# lands where it says. The other half of the confusion is -R, which defers key
# generation to the first connection -- so the directory stays empty until someone
# actually connects, and an empty directory is not evidence of a problem.
#
# This key is currently on a filesystem with no permission bits, so it is
# readable by anyone holding the card. Do not reuse it anywhere that matters, and
# note that the real fix is a root password rather than a better filesystem.

. /etc/zlyme.conf

# -B: root has an empty password. Without this, sshd rejects the
# development login that serial already allows.
DROPBEAR_ARGS="-B -r ${ZLYME_CFG}/dropbear/dropbear_ed25519_host_key"
